Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is 'financial point of view' the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.4. Evaluation of Proposals. <omitted> (f) The Company Board may, at any time prior to obtaining Stockholder Approval, declare an Acquisition Proposal to be a Superior Proposal if, and only if, the Company Board, after extensive consultation with its outside legal counsel and a financial advisor of nationally recognized reputation, determines in good faith that the proposal offers superior financial benefits and aligns with the strategic objectives of the Company. Furthermore, the Board must ensure that the proposal complies with fiduciary duties under applicable Law and does not contravene any existing agreements. The Company must provide the Parent with a detailed written notice of its intention to consider the proposal superior, including a comprehensive evaluation of the financial and strategic merits of the proposal. The Company shall engage in good faith negotiations with the Parent during a period of five Business Days to allow for potential revisions to the existing proposal, ensuring it meets the Company's strategic goals and fiduciary responsibilities.\nAnswer:
