Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 8.2. Consideration of Proposals. <omitted> (f) The Company Board, in evaluating any Acquisition Proposal, must determine in good faith, after consultation with the Company’s outside legal counsel and financial advisors, whether such proposal is a Superior Proposal. This determination must take into account not only the financial terms but also the strategic benefits, potential synergies, long-term impacts on shareholder value, and compliance with fiduciary duties under applicable Law. The Company Board shall consider (i) the likelihood of the proposal being completed, (ii) the impact on the Company's reputation, and (iii) the alignment with the Company's strategic objectives. <omitted> (g) The Company shall engage in good faith negotiations with the Parent to allow for any amendments to the terms and conditions of the current Agreement to potentially enhance its attractiveness. (Page 88)\nAnswer:
