Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 8.3. Evaluation of Acquisition Proposals. <omitted> (c) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, the Company Board may change its recommendation if, and only if, (i) it determines in good faith, after consultation with the Company’s financial advisor, that an Acquisition Proposal constitutes a Superior Proposal, (ii) the Company has provided written notice to the Parent of its intention to change the recommendation, including all material terms of the Acquisition Proposal, and (iii) after considering the advice of its outside legal counsel and the implications of its fiduciary duties, the Company Board determines that failing to change its recommendation would be inconsistent with its fiduciary duties under applicable Law. Additionally, the Company shall negotiate in good faith with the Parent to amend the terms of this Agreement so that the Acquisition Proposal no longer constitutes a Superior Proposal. Any revisions to the financial terms or other material terms of the Acquisition Proposal will require a new determination by the Company Board. (Pages 45-46)\nAnswer:
